# Fixed 2026-11-25, defect d-lane-d2745-1's class, lane-ctx-after: a save point fills a context
# (`getcontext`) in a static page made unreadable with `mprotect` right after it returns, the
# program never jumps to it, and another save point and a signal's handler follow. Correct C.
# Symptom: built with `HERO_JMP_FORCE_UNCALIBRATED` 3, the runtime faulted at the next save point,
# SIGSEGV on Linux arm64 (exit 139) and a Bus error on this Mac (exit 138).
# Cause: route A, the record the runtime keeps of the save points where the C library's layout
# does not verify, fingerprints a buffer saved before at the next save point and at a handler's
# begin, wherever the buffer lies in memory that provably lives (the thread's stack, an image
# loaded before `main`, the alternate stack), reading it by a load of its own: memory that
# lives can still be made unreadable.
# Fix: the fingerprint's bytes are copied by the kernel (`process_vm_readv` on Linux,
# `mach_vm_read_overwrite` on Darwin), and a copy refused is a buffer read as unreadable, never a
# fault. Route A is not this platform's route on the CI's legs, so the forced builds
# (`HERO_JMP_FORCE_UNCALIBRATED` 3) are run apart (a golden carries no define); this case holds
# what both routes must say.
extern "fixedbugs-a-saved-buffer-made-unreadable-before-the-next-save.h"
    function ur_run(how: i32) -> i64

function main()
    print("ur_1: ", ur_run(how: 1))
